//? Title: Text Tokenization: Ranked Merges, Greedy Pieces and Padding
//? Subtitle: Vocabulary fitting, unknown text and padding masks are separate decisions
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Variable-length text must become integer sequences without confusing real tokens, unknown words
and padding. We compare small learned byte-pair merges with a fixed WordPiece vocabulary and
inspect right truncation and padding. Unicode validation and model serialization are checked
separately from any language-model quality claim.
)
\toc

> The method

Whitespace tokenization preserves case and punctuation and splits on ASCII whitespace.
Character tokenization validates UTF-8 and returns Unicode scalar values, not grapheme clusters.
BPE starts with characters plus an end-of-word marker, repeatedly merges the most frequent adjacent
pair weighted by word counts, and breaks ties lexicographically. Encoding applies learned merges in
rank order. With pair count c(p), each training step chooses

$$
p^*=\arg\max_p c(p),\qquad c(p)=\sum_w f(w)\,\#\{p\text{ adjacent in }w\}.
$$
\alttext(The selected adjacent pair maximises frequency weighted by each corpus word count.)

The reference implementation rescans the corpus each step, targeting small corpora. WordPiece
instead uses a supplied vocabulary and greedy longest prefixes with ## continuation pieces. If
any suffix fails, the whole word becomes UNK. IDs follow supplied vocabulary order, with PAD=0
and UNK=1. PadTokens right-truncates, right-pads and emits a separate Float32 mask. Empty rows have
zero mask mass; a masked mean must guard its denominator. Neither tokenizer normalises Unicode,
lowercases text or learns the WordPiece vocabulary.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/text_tokenization_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/text_tokenization_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/text_tokenization_concept.tikz > build/text_tokenization_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/text_tokenization_concept.svg build/text_tokenization_concept.dvi > build/text_tokenization_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/text_tokenization_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/text_tokenization_concept.svg from local TikZ source.
// \image(figures/text_tokenization_concept.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Vocabulary fitting, unknown text and padding masks are separate decisions)
\alttext(Training word counts; or fixed vocabulary Then Ranked BPE merges; or greedy WordPiece Then IDs + padding mask; PAD=0; UNK=1.)

> Simple examples

Counts aaa:3, ab:1 make (a,a) the first BPE merge. A fixed vocabulary splits playing into play and ##ing, while playx becomes one unknown word.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/text_tokenization_masks.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace t=dm::nn::data::text;
 const auto b=t::Bpe::Train({{"aaa",3},{"ab",1}},1);
 const t::WordPiece wp({"[PAD]","[UNK]","play","##ing","ball"});
 const auto batch=t::PadTokens({wp.Encode("playing ball"),wp.Encode("playx"),{}},4);
 dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;std::vector<double> row,length;std::vector<std::string> pieces;
 for(const auto& word:{"playing","playx","ball"}){std::string s;for(const auto& p:wp.EncodeWord(word)){if(!s.empty())s+=" | ";s+=p;}pieces.push_back(s);}
 for(int i=0;i<3;++i){row.push_back(i);length.push_back(dm::nn::sum(dm::nn::take_rows(batch.mask,{i})).item());}
 table.add_column("word",std::vector<std::string>{"playing","playx","ball"});table.add_column("pieces",pieces);
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.bars(row,length,"unmasked tokens",{37,99,235},.6)
 .title("Padding does not add real tokens").x_label("sentence: playing ball, playx, empty").y_label("mask mass").y_limits(0,4);
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");p.size(850,420).save_svg("build/text_tokenization_masks.svg");
 if(b.merges()[0]!=t::Bpe::Pair{"a","a"}||length!=std::vector<double>({3,1,0}))return 1;
 std::print("First BPE merge: {} + {}.\n\n{}\n",b.merges()[0].first,b.merges()[0].second,table.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
First BPE merge: a + a.

| word | pieces |
| :--- | :--- |
| playing | play \| ##ing |
| playx | [UNK] |
| ball | ball |

\image(figures/text_tokenization_masks.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Known subwords, one whole-word UNK and empty input have different mask lengths.)
\alttext(Known subwords, one whole-word UNK and empty input have different mask lengths.)

> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace t=dm::nn::data::text;
 const auto b=t::Bpe::Train({{"low",5},{"lower",2},{"newest",6},{"widest",3}},12),copy=t::Bpe::Load(b.Save());
 int checked=0;for(const auto w:{"low","lower","lowest","newest","z"}){if(b.EncodeWord(w)!=copy.EncodeWord(w))return 1;++checked;}
 int rejected=0;for(const std::string bad:{std::string("\xc0\x80",2),std::string("\xed\xa0\x80",3),std::string("\xf4\x90\x80\x80",4)})try{t::Characters(bad);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
 const auto truncated=t::PadTokens({{2,3,4,5,6}},3);
 if(rejected!=3||truncated.ids.to_vector<int>()!=std::vector<int>({2,3,4}))return 1;
 std::print("Serialized BPE: {} ranked merges; {} encodings exactly preserved.\n\nInvalid UTF-8 cases rejected: {}; right-truncation retains three IDs.\n",b.merges().size(),checked,rejected);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Serialized BPE: 12 ranked merges; 5 encodings exactly preserved.

Invalid UTF-8 cases rejected: 3; right-truncation retains three IDs.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The learned merge order survives serialization, WordPiece's unknown rule applies to whole words,
and the mask distinguishes padding from actual IDs. Corpus, vocabulary order, normalisation policy,
merge budget and truncation length are part of a reproducible text pipeline.

> References

- [Sennrich, Haddow and Birch (2016), Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units](https://aclanthology.org/P16-1162/).
- [BERT WordPiece tokenizer source](https://github.com/google-research/bert/blob/master/tokenization.py).
